-- Slice 6 (proof caps), the LEGIT MINT accept -- the sanctioned way to
-- construct a proof cap: `Db`'s own PUBLIC (`fn`) `run_migrations` mints its
-- own `Cap(Db.Migrated)` via `mint_cap`. `mint_cap(x) : Cap(P)` typechecks iff
-- `P`'s declaring module == the enclosing module AND the enclosing fn is
-- public (`fn`, not `pfn`); `Db` satisfies both, so the mint is allowed. Check
-- 6's self-declaration exemption also lets `Db`'s public fns return their own
-- proof cap, and a proof cap's declaring module implicitly satisfies its own
-- `needs` (self-declaration, Check 1), so `Db` needs no `needs Db.Migrated`
-- line. This is the accept counterpart to `reject/t56` (external mint) and
-- `reject/t57` (pfn mint) -- the mint surface is exactly the declaring
-- module's public fns. `mint_cap` is runtime-erased (it aliases `cap_narrow`
-- for all downstream passes), so this has zero runtime effect. See
-- core-march-types.md §2.8.13.
mod Db do
  proof cap Migrated
  needs IO

  fn run_migrations(cap : Cap(IO)) : Cap(Db.Migrated) do
    mint_cap(cap)
  end
end
